TI  - Role of chitinase in the pathogenicity of Beauveria bassiana against the rice earhead bug Leptocorisa oratorius (Order: Hemiptera; Family: Alydidae)
AN  - PPR1263329
DO  - 10.21203/rs.3.rs-10067778/v1
AB  - Abstract Chitinase is a key virulence factor in entomopathogenic fungi, enabling host invasion through degradation of the chitin-rich insect cuticle. This study evaluated the role of chitinase produced by a native isolate of Beauveria bassiana (Bals.) Vuill. (Hypocreales; Cordycipitaceae) against the rice earhead bug, Leptocorisa oratorius (Fab.) (Hemiptera: Alydidae). Laboratory bioassays showed stage-dependent susceptibility, with first-instar nymphs exhibiting the highest mortality (84%) at 9 days after treatment, while egg hatchability was reduced to 72%, indicating partial ovicidal activity. Chitinase production was optimized under different cultural and environmental conditions, with maximum activity observed on the fifth day of incubation. Peptone (0.07%), colloidal chitin (0.4%), and FeSO4 (0.015%) significantly enhanced enzyme production. Optimal fungal growth occurred at pH 5 and 28C, whereas maximum chitinase activity was recorded at pH 5 and 37C. Partial purification by ammonium sulfate fractionation resulted in the highest specific activity (2.60 U mg1), purification fold (2.68), and yield (14.34%) at 75% saturation. SDS-PAGE analysis revealed a molecular weight of approximately 60 kDa. The results confirm the importance of chitinase in B. bassiana pathogenicity and highlight its potential for developing eco-friendly biopesticides for sustainable management of rice insect pests.
