AB  - BackgroundThis study investigates the gene expression dynamics and biocontrol effectiveness of Beauveria bassiana against Spodoptera frugiperda, the fall armyworm, a notable agricultural pest. Our objectives were to analyze the B. bassiana gene expression variation during insect infection compared to grow on artificial media and to evaluate the effects of different spore concentrations on larval mortality, development, and reproduction.MethodsA combination of bioassays and transcriptome analysis was employed. S. frugiperda larvae were exposed to different spore concentrations, and mortality rates were recorded at various developmental stages. RNA sequencing was performed on fungal samples from infected larvae and those grown on 1/4-strength Sabouraud Dextrose Agar with Yeast Extract (SDAY) media. Differential gene expression libraries were constructed at 48, 96, and 144 hours' post-infection. Gene Ontology (GO) and Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) pathway analyses were used to identification of biological processes and pathways that differentiate infection from growth on artificial media.ResultsThe highest spore concentration (1 107 spores/mL) significantly increased larval mortality, prolonged developmental stages, and reduced reproductive success, particularly in pupation, adult emergence, and female fecundity. Transcriptomic analysis revealed substantial differences in gene expression between
